WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.960
Is the map already in the dividing cells, or is it brought in by the axons that

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:08.960
arrive?

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.550
Two answers were proposed a year apart.

4
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:16.053
In 1988, Pasko Rakic proposed the protomap.

5
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:22.147
Cortical progenitors lie in the ventricular zone, the layer of the brain wall next

6
00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:23.507
to the fluid-filled ventricle.

7
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:29.757
Newborn neurons climb outward along radial glia, long scaffold cells that span the

8
00:00:29.757 --> 00:00:30.757
wall.

9
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:34.407
So neurons born from one patch stack up in a column above it.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.220
If the patches already differ by position, the outline of the areas is laid down

11
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.320
where the cells are made.

12
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.340
Think of a city zoned into districts before a single house is built.

13
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.050
In 1989, Dennis O'Leary proposed the protocortex.

14
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.480
Here the young sheet has broadly similar potential everywhere.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:00.573
Areas emerge mainly from the axons that arrive from the thalamus.

16
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:05.643
Different thalamic nuclei send axons to different parts of the sheet.

17
00:01:06.503 --> 00:01:08.423
The input tells each part what to become.

18
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:14.533
Suppose you found molecular differences across the progenitors, and a map that forms

19
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:15.913
without thalamic input.

20
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:18.073
Which idea would that support?

21
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:27.397
The protomap. That is what it predicts.

22
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.567
The protocortex predicts something else.

23
00:01:31.447 --> 00:01:35.367
Young cortex given new input should take on features that match the input.

24
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:38.367
Both predictions came true in part.

25
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.707
So the field now treats the map as drawn twice.

26
00:01:42.607 --> 00:01:45.927
First a sketch from the sheet, then a copy refined by its inputs.

27
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:51.167
One more thing before any result: how do you read a map in a newborn mouse?

28
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:58.320
Mice lacking Emx2, and mice lacking working Pax6, die around birth, before the areas

29
00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.320
mature.

30
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:02.463
So an area in these experiments means a proxy.

31
00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:05.577
First proxy: marker genes.

32
00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:09.177
A stain shows where a gene is switched on in a tissue section.

33
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.670
Some genes have borders at birth that line up with future areas.

34
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.370
Second proxy: connections.

35
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.670
A thalamic nucleus is a cluster of neurons.

36
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.290
Each one projects mainly to its own part of cortex.

37
00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:29.273
Dye placed in one spot of cortex labels the thalamic neurons that project there,

38
00:02:29.773 --> 00:02:31.633
sometimes in more than one nucleus.

39
00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:34.776
Third proxy: the barrel field.

40
00:02:34.776 --> 00:02:40.416
In mice that survive past birth, a stain on flattened cortex shows the barrels as

41
00:02:40.416 --> 00:02:41.416
dark patches.

42
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.050
In mice that die at birth, these proxies stand in for mature areas.

43
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.150
Animals that survive allow later and adult measures too.

44
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:56.213
The takeaway: the two ideas make different predictions, and early proxies are one

45
00:02:56.213 --> 00:02:56.993
way to test them.
